  TRAIT
    COMMENT "/// A type that be used as part of a cache key."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// A cache looks up artefacts by a cache key. Many cache keys are composed of sub-keys. For example,"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// caching the lint results of a file depend at least on the file content, the user settings, and linter version."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// Types implementing the [`CacheKey`] trait can be used as part of a cache key by which artefacts are queried."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ## Implementing `CacheKey`"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// You can derive [`CacheKey`] with `#[derive(CacheKey)]` if all fields implement [`CacheKey`]. The resulting"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// cache key will be the combination of the values from calling `cache_key` on each field."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ```"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// # use ruff_macros::CacheKey;"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// #[derive(CacheKey)]"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// struct Test {"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///     name: String,"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///     version: u32,"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// }"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ```"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// If you need more control over computing the cache key, you can of course implement the [`CacheKey]` yourself:"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ```"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///  use ruff_cache::{CacheKey, CacheKeyHasher};"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// struct Test {"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///     name: String,"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///     version: u32,"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///     other: String"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// }"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// impl CacheKey for Test {"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///     fn cache_key(&self, state: &mut CacheKeyHasher) {"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///         self.name.cache_key(state);"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///         self.version.cache_key(state);"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///     }"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// }"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ```"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ## Portability"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// Ideally, the cache key is portable across platforms but this is not yet a strict requirement."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ## Using [`Hash`]"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// You can defer to the [`Hash`] implementation for non-composite types."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// Be aware, that the [`Hash`] implementation may not be portable."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// ## Why a new trait rather than reusing [`Hash`]?"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// The main reason is that hashes and cache keys have different constraints:"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// * Cache keys are less performance sensitive: Hashes must be super fast to compute for performant hashed-collections. That's"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///   why some standard types don't implement [`Hash`] where it would be safe to implement [`CacheKey`], e.g. `HashSet`"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// * Cache keys must be deterministic where hash keys do not have this constraint. That's why pointers don't implement [`CacheKey`] but they implement [`Hash`]."
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// * Ideally, cache keys are portable"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "///"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    COMMENT "/// [`Hash`](Hash)"
    WHITESPACE "\n"
    VISIBILITY
